tsrs-only: the session refers to its server weakly, so that dropping the last `Arc<Server>` frees the session and
everything it owns (snapshots, projects, program owners, checker and file regions). Go's session holds the `*Server`
as its `project.Client` and `ata.NpmExecutor` and the GC collects the cycle; in tsrs the server owns the session
(`Server.session`) and the session held `Arc<Server>` twice, so an in-process server (`lsptestutil`, which the Rust
fourslash stand-in `crates/ets_fourslash` uses once per baseline case) was never freed: TestEffectQuickFixes grew by
~60 MB per case (23.5 GB peak for 385 cases, 54.7 GB for the whole baseline suite).

- tsrs_lsp/src/server.rs: `sessionClient(Weak<Server>)` implements `project::Client` and `project::NpmExecutor`
  by forwarding to the server while it is alive (as the logger already holds `Weak<Server>`); once the server is
  gone its methods do nothing (`Ok(())`, `false`, `Locale::DEFAULT`; `npm_install` fails). `handle_initialized`
  passes it to `new_session` instead of `Arc<Server>`. No behavior change while the server is alive (the server
  outlives the session in the binary: `tsrs --lsp` holds it until the process exits).
- tsrs_lsp/src/server_test.rs: `a_closed_client_frees_the_server_and_its_session` (fails without the change).

